ha256" "b065f9972521b589059d85837013f478d516446f49d5b266bdbd017a28cd5896"} {"question" "q-78570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=AB\\,d"] ["math" "x=BA\\,d"] ["math" "x=B\\,d\\,A"] ["math" "x=B\\,d\\,{A}^{-1}"] ["math" "x={A}^{-1}B\\,d"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995994/state.json" "file_sha256" "b065f9972521b589059d85837013f478d516446f49d5b266bdbd017a28cd5896"} {"question" "q-78570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=AB\\,d"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995994/state.json" "file_sha256" "b065f9972521b589059d85837013f478d516446f49d5b266bdbd017a28cd5896"} {"question" "q-78571" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider a matrix equation $A\\,x={B}^{-1}\\,d,$ where $A$ and $B$ are $2\\times 2$ non-singular matrices, while $x$ and $d$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors. Solve the equation for $x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995994/state.json" "file_sha256" "b065f9972521b589059d85837013f478d516446f49d5b266bdbd017a28cd5896"} {"question" "q-78571" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $A$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${A}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $A\\,x={B}^{-1}\\,d$ by ${A}^{-1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\,x & ={B}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ {A}^{-1}A\\,x & ={A}^{-1}{B}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ {I}_{2}\\,x & ={A}^{-1}{B}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}{B}^{-1}\\,d.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995994/state.json" "file_sha256" "b065f9972521b589059d85837013f478d516446f49d5b266bdbd017a28cd5896"} {"question" "q-78571" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995994/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a25c47371cb5ede8ec6ee3fdcdcd7c8a35474135dbd8aff72edb68649a8c7c23"} {"question" "q-78571" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995994/state.json" "file_sha256" "b065f9972521b589059d85837013f478d516446f49d5b266bdbd017a28cd5896"} {"question" "q-78571" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=A{B}^{-1}\\,d"] ["math" "x={A}^{-1}{B}^{-1}\\,d"] ["math" "x={B}^{-1}\\,d\\,A"] ["math" "x={B}^{-1}\\,d\\,{A}^{-1}"] ["math" "x={B}^{-1}{A}^{-1}\\,d"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995994/state.json" "file_sha256" "b065f9972521b589059d85837013f478d516446f49d5b266bdbd017a28cd5896"} {"question" "q-78571" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x={A}^{-1}{B}^{-1}\\,d"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995994/state.json" "file_sha256" "b065f9972521b589059d85837013f478d516446f49d5b266bdbd017a28cd5896"} {"question" "e-4520" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2x+5y-2z=-2 \\\\ -3y+z=-2 \\\\ 3x-7y+3z=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Given that $\\mathbf x = \\langle x,y,z\\rangle$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995994/example-4520.json" "file_sha256" "4b964b7c03e97a8bf8af4cc8d124ec6130966bfd79fd36f3bd67246aa35667f2"} {"question" "e-4520" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -7 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is given by $\\mathbf{x}= A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=3, y=0, z=-2.$\n\nFinally, $x+y+z=3+0+(-2)=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995994/example-4520.json" "file_sha256" "4b964b7c03e97a8bf8af4cc8d124ec6130966bfd79fd36f3bd67246aa35667f2"} {"question" "e-4521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the following system of linear equations using matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3y+x+z=0 \\\\ z+4y-5=2 \\\\ 1+2x-y=-3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995994/example-4521.json" "file_sha256" "be553980ec8cec33fd10c27c0e9f4cf2ef8fc4ab564cbe596ba93edddc265b14"} {"question" "e-4521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we organize the system so that the variables are in the correct order and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x+3y+z=0 \\\\ 0x+4y+z=7 \\\\ 2x-y+0z=-4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we  write the system in the form $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is given by $\\mathbf{x}= A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we compute $A^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1*: Find the determinant of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(1)-3(-2)+(-8) \\\\  & =1+6-8 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2*: Compute the matrix of minors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3*:  Find the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -8 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n*Step 4*: Transpose the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 7 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n*Step 5*: Apply the inverse matrix formula.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 7 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate the solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -33\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=3,$ $y=10,$ $z=-33.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995994/example-4521.json" "file_sha256" "be553980ec8cec33fd10c27c0e9f4cf2ef8fc4ab564cbe596ba93edddc265b14"} {"question" "e-4604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider a matrix equation $B\\,\\mathbf{x} = A^{-1}\\,\\mathbf{d},$ where $A$ and $B$ are $10 \\times 10$ non-singular matrices, while $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{d}$ are $10 \\times 1$ column vectors. Solve the equation for $\\mathbf{x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995994/example-4604.json" "file_sha256" "7f11d799b12cb75f7411868211e4d49d5dbc09e704590655d9074f0567510cf7"} {"question" "e-4604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B$ is not singular, it must have an inverse $B^{−1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $B\\,\\mathbf{x} = A^{-1}\\,\\mathbf{d}$ by $B^{-1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ {B}^{-1}B\\,x & ={B}^{-1}{A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ {I}_{10}\\,x & ={B}^{-1}{A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ x & ={B}^{-1}{A}^{-1}\\,d.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995994/example-4604.json" "file_sha256" "7f11d799b12cb75f7411868211e4d49d5dbc09e704590655d9074f0567510cf7"}] "usage" {"q-42449" [] "e-4521" [] "q-78442" [] "q-78557" [] "e-4520" [] "e-4604" [] "q-9722" []} "basis" 1064 "retractions" [[17592186092764 :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n${x - y + 3z = 2; x - 2y - 5z =-1; 2x - 3y + 2z = 1$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax = b$. Given that $x = ⟨x, y, z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $x + y + z$?\n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n$A^{-1} =-\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-19 & -7 & 11 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186092656 :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n${2x - 3 + z = 0; 5y - 1 + 3x = 2z + 1; 3z + 2x - 1 = 2y$\nFind $x + y + z$."] [17592186092884 :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n${2y + 3z + x =-1; 4z + y = 1; 5x + 6y - 3 =-3$"] [17592186092759 :question/problem "Consider a system of $3$ linear equations in $3$ variables, written in matrix form as $Dy = w$. Here, $D$ is the non-singular coefficient matrix of the system, $y$ is the column vector containing the variables, and $w$ is the column vector containing the constants. The unique solution of the system can be found using the method shown below:\n\n$Dy|= w; D^{-1}Dy|= D - 1w; I_{3}y|= D^{-1}w; D|= D^{-1}w$\n\nFrom top to bottom and left to right, what are the missing parts of the derivation?"] [17592186091972 :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n${-2x + 5y - 2z =-2; - 3y + z =-2; 3x - 7y + 3z = 3$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$. Given that $\\mathbf x = \\langle x,y,z\\rangle$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z$?\n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186091972 :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAx &= b \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -7 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}= A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= A^{-1}b \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is\n\n$$\nx=3, y=0, z=-2\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx+y+z=3+0+(-2)=1\n$$"] [17592186091973 :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations using matrices:\n${3y + x + z = 0; z + 4y - 5 = 2; 1 + 2x - y =-3$"] [17592186091973 :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that the variables are in the correct order and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n{x + 3y + z = 0; 0x + 4y + z = 7; 2x - y + 0z =-4\n$$\n\nNext, we write the system in the form\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\n, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}= A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute $A^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1*: Find the determinant of $A$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A) =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} &= 1\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}- 3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+ 1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (1) - 3(-2) + (-8) \\\\\n&= 1 + 6 - 8 \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2*: Compute the matrix of minors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= [\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\mid \\mid 0\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid \\mid \\mid 0\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}- 1 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\mid \\mid 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid \\mid \\mid 1\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}- 1 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\mid \\mid 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{vmatrix}1 \\mid \\mid \\mid 1\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid ] \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3*: Find the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -8 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n*Step 4*: Transpose the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 7 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n*Step 5*: Apply the inverse matrix formula.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 7 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate the solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= A^{-1}b \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -33\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=3$, $y=10$, $z=-33$."] [17592186091974 :question/problem "Consider a matrix equation $B\\,\\mathbf{x} = A^{-1}\\,\\mathbf{d}$, where $A$ and $B$ are $10 \\times 10$ non-singular matrices, while $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{d}$ are $10 \\times 1$ column vectors. Solve the equation for $\\mathbf{x}$."] [17592186091974 :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ is not singular, it must have an inverse $B^{-1}$. Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation\n\n$$\nB\\,\\mathbf{x} = A^{-1}\\,\\mathbf{d}\n$$\n\nby $B^{-1}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBx &= A^{-1}d \\\\\nB^{-1}Bx &= B^{-1}A^{-1}d \\\\\nI_{10}x &= B^{-1}A^{-1}d \\\\\nx &= B^{-1}A^{-1}d\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186090402 17592186090403 17592186090404 17592186090405 17592186090406 17592186091020 17592186091021 17592186091022 17592186091023 17592186091024 17592186091050 17592186091051 17592186091052 17592186091053 17592186091054 17592186091770 17592186091771 17592186091772 17592186091773 17592186091774] "immutable_fields" [[17592186090401 :answer-field/correct] [17592186090401 :answer-field/id] [17592186090401 :answer-field/key] [17592186090401 :answer-field/type] [17592186091019 :answer-field/correct] [17592186091019 :answer-field/id] [17592186091019 :answer-field/key] [17592186091019 :answer-field/type] [17592186091049 :answer-field/correct] [17592186091049 :answer-field/id] [17592186091049 :answer-field/key] [17592186091049 :answer-field/type] [17592186091769 :answer-field/correct] [17592186091769 :answer-field/id] [17592186091769 :answer-field/key] [17592186091769 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
